// Read validator 2's REV balance **after** its quarantine payout — the same read as
// `examples/leave-balance.rho`, on a **different channel**.
//
// **Why a second file for one different string.** `listen-data-at-name` returns whatever is already at
// the name, and only waits if there is nothing there. It is *not* a subscribe-to-changes read, which is
// what the leave rig's first attempt assumed. So a name reused across two reads hands the second read the
// **first read's datum** — the 2026-10-04 run reported `0 -> 0` for an account that had been funded
// 100,000,000, and the stale datum is identifiable in the probe's own output: its `block_number` was 5,
// three blocks before the fund and nine before the withdraw. A distinct channel per read is what makes
// the read a read.
//
// The before-half of the pair is `leave-balance.rho`; the rig takes it after the `withdraw` deploy, so
// the difference between the two reads is the payout and nothing else: validator 2 makes no deploy of
// its own after the withdraw, the payout is the only credit its vault can receive, and both reads are
// signed by the **genesis** deployer, whose phlo never touches the balance being read.
//
//   tools/devnet.sh deploy leave-balance-after.rho && tools/devnet.sh query leave-balance-after
new revAddr(`rho:rev:address`), vault(`rho:rchain:revVault`), ret1, ret2 in {
  revAddr!("fromPublicKey", "04d8b6c325ae12e89823866b2a292a62d7acee520954761890a1621fef79dca1c8e8df79dd8519480e5c015ae6cf3ba7de8669e260561616a36eb9c308b5983ab0".hexToBytes(), *ret1) |
  for (@addr <- ret1) {
    vault!("getBalance", addr, *ret2) |
    for (@bal <- ret2) {
      @"leave-balance-after"!(bal)
    }
  }
}
